{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "F41AnAssertionCannotSayWhyItIsRight",
    "description": "REPRO, EXPECTED TO FAIL. An `assertions` entry accepts NO annotation key. This document is `docs/findings/F13_enum_leaf_one.esm` -- which validates and passes -- with one `description` added to its single assertion, and nothing else changed. `esm validate` refuses it:\n\n    /models/F41AnAssertionCannotSayWhyItIsRight/tests/0/assertions/0:\n        Additional properties are not allowed ('description' was unexpected)\n\nSix spellings were tried against the pinned toolchain and all six are refused, so the obstacle is the closed property list rather than a misremembered key name: `description`, `_comment`, `comment`, `note`, `label`, `rationale`. Removing the key makes the same document validate and pass, which is what attributes the refusal to the annotation and not to anything else here.\n\nWHY THIS PROJECT CARES. docs/esm-conventions.md 35.4 is a rule about assertions: say which of them face the REFERENCE and which face the PORT, `in the test rather than only in the specification, because a reader who reaches the assertions first is the reader most likely to draw the wrong conclusion from them`. The format has nowhere to put that sentence. A test carries one `description` for all of its assertions, so a document that follows 35.4 has to write an ordinal-addressed list -- `[7] rt_so2Factor: THE PORT'S OWN ARITHMETIC` -- inside the test's prose and keep it aligned by hand. fixtures/chain-so2-co2e-mechanism.esm carries three such lists over 45 assertions.\n\nTHE COST IS NOT THE TYPING, IT IS THAT THE LIST GOES STALE SILENTLY. Inserting an assertion renumbers every one after it and nothing checks the list; a per-assertion field could not drift from the assertion it is attached to. That is the same failure mode `tools/check-sources.py` was given a rule for when eight `metadata.note`s travelled with copied structure and described the wrong snapshot -- and there the note lives ON the thing it describes and could still be checked against it, which an ordinal list cannot.\n\nWHAT WOULD FIX IT: any optional string property on an assertion. The tripwire reads a passing repro as `the defect is fixed`, and this one both validates and passes the day one exists.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "finding",
      "tests",
      "assertions",
      "documentation"
    ]
  },
  "enums": {
    "probe": {
      "Symbol": 1
    }
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "f41_rows": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 1
    }
  },
  "models": {
